enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24cfcda8-afbc-5331-885b-bcfa05c0d741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424301} {:answer/id #uuid "5553ca7e-bc04-53a9-ab03-d33a73524410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424307} {:answer/id #uuid "778dd242-bc8c-5163-ba5e-b482fc9a6724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424310} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdc8ff4-a08f-5057-bc05-25918e661b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424314} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4cbdd3-75cd-5bc5-8f98-2159597bf317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5553ca7e-bc04-53a9-ab03-d33a73524410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424307} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf8a023-f586-55e1-889c-e57ed171c509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff6bf6f1-3574-59b2-8046-e358528c7f1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83946" :question/problem "![](images/31/31054ef4614814dd1c9131192504ea37f3527af75d8292fbce943c16235c9303.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A$ \nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef340c0-cff5-5bfa-b9a1-2e0d4a2e3f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef340c0-cff5-5bfa-b9a1-2e0d4a2e3f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289190} :answer-field/id #uuid "ada5e9bd-53a7-576a-8573-bd6cafce876c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f6fbb46-cecc-525a-94f8-20e139099f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6fbb46-cecc-525a-94f8-20e139099f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288993} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5397a8-9380-5e98-988d-5860f188cfc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3ec1edd-6bd3-59bd-aa3e-85aa30d0d59b" :question/math-academy-id "q-237947" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x+6){4}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-2.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b79a8b4a-d9da-5751-bf84-2ea3ac2d8273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156670} {:answer/id #uuid "f85332da-a474-5cf9-903e-8e53b94a1db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156671} {:answer/id #uuid "0110d702-1341-5795-91fe-96cb9ceaa747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156672} {:answer/id #uuid "12de591b-78ef-5ad8-9892-6ab882ff2df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156673} {:answer/id #uuid "35be39b5-25de-52f5-bc1e-438660dea9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156674}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e8aae86d-fa5b-56b3-9840-439682d2e9e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156669}] :question/id #uuid "4d4d55ac-1783-5c4f-86d0-b4c81eca4662" :question/math-academy-id "q-88412" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^{2}e^{2x} - 9e^{2x} = 0$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8c0a16-b911-5fe8-a804-c6535aeb4230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235897} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0e3382-f5f0-5032-9112-9a9a3b0a092d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236101} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd55ce7-abc9-5136-8252-a8c9889c341c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236226} {:answer/id #uuid "547d930b-f494-57d5-9e2a-db7366bef16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236355} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3ba58b-f375-5901-a123-b25fc65695af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea3ba58b-f375-5901-a123-b25fc65695af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237287} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e46ebc-53b2-5521-88ed-68aaf1f4f829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19bca5be-bffd-5da5-b2f8-88bf9c5fec74" :question/math-academy-id "q-100781" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8x+12){7}^{x-3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x-3}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x-3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=2,6,$ and their product is $2\\cdot 6=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b49158d-1e9b-5620-aa68-4bc86800e77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235985} {:answer/id #uuid "402eb34a-619c-5c28-b06d-a763be8a0260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236227} {:answer/id #uuid "89710b44-592c-5600-8225-1ded09fa8b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236678} {:answer/id #uuid "8cedd7cd-8213-5fa6-a8a1-aab66b50379a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236708} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4daebb-fc52-5b89-9526-438c8a3b6213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b49158d-1e9b-5620-aa68-4bc86800e77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235985} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda41c81-3c6e-51fc-b6bb-6060a77b012b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "731d88c0-04ba-5bfa-af89-56694fda5912" :question/math-academy-id "q-100785" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-36){e}^{9x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{9x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+6)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{9x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{9x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-6,6,$ and their product is $(-6)\\cdot 6=-36.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b298d90-e2b4-5471-976c-b8ed9690b02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b298d90-e2b4-5471-976c-b8ed9690b02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288204} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf610bb3-bb99-5ba5-937a-a2246750dd7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d10c43-57f4-5ac0-9f7d-8bebd2d13863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d10c43-57f4-5ac0-9f7d-8bebd2d13863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebecd072-9bc9-56ba-be2a-35c69511a3f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa483285-7fb1-55f0-9e07-a601fc5a4d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-237946" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6c3f287-5dae-5601-8ebb-1ede42e5ca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c3f287-5dae-5601-8ebb-1ede42e5ca46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46a4763-9986-5fe1-adec-09a176e52d03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7541a123-c295-5764-92f6-cc633d5666e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7541a123-c295-5764-92f6-cc633d5666e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291133} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77cc2d2-1e8e-508d-83cc-6c1e4f0ee7f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6bdd7ef-3aa2-50b8-bd67-7fd5c008275b" :question/math-academy-id "q-264754" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2x}-50\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {7}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)(x+5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d579f97-0ec1-5880-be03-82e505ce65d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d579f97-0ec1-5880-be03-82e505ce65d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290753} :answer-field/id #uuid "accc6009-df01-5cf7-b489-f8abf6c8772b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292273} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8c0005f-1c7c-58e1-8ace-35c2f623dd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c0005f-1c7c-58e1-8ace-35c2f623dd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291682} :answer-field/id #uuid "daaa6d02-1f99-53bf-abf0-10dac7839c78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f62fb8d-cfd4-5a86-8152-bac3c04d2838" :question/math-academy-id "q-264772" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x}+4x\\cdot {3}^{x}-48\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+4x-48){3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+4x-48=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+2x-24) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+6)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77c3cd4-4822-5b88-b531-15ff6b1e643c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e319a330-863d-5a2b-83b4-4b5600e073c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292441}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77ac52fb-b13e-5bb0-9619-5d3b00902a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-264778" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a78efb5-52b6-5d3c-b5a0-49f39f6da8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a78efb5-52b6-5d3c-b5a0-49f39f6da8ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291022} :answer-field/id #uuid "818baac4-9846-582e-a016-d7c37e9a7e42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37e3585-e3ba-5017-8c94-4dbab9c04a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a37e3585-e3ba-5017-8c94-4dbab9c04a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291345} :answer-field/id #uuid "885f1080-4f56-5ac6-a8d7-1a1b1f221f7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f911a188-0d00-5124-b074-90cbb871a900" :question/math-academy-id "q-264780" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+11x\\cdot {5}^{2x+1}+28\\cdot {5}^{2x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+11x+28){5}^{2x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+11x+28=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{2x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+11x+28=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+7)(x+4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-7,-4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{2x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{2x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-7\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292ef02d-b7cd-5a35-82b0-cbb44129f1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351949} {:answer/id #uuid "871be151-8a04-536c-bbbf-3c044fc1b69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352749} {:answer/id #uuid "9121608d-7af0-59ef-b803-1e106a41ad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352825} {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fb693c-d101-5072-82da-c04f62a50942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34b0cee-72c8-5bf5-989a-5a931ef5ee95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353547} :answer-field/id #uuid "659dbad8-622b-5bc5-99b1-3e39b5187855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354004}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ebf2795-4a5b-5fc4-bae2-4108180a784e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88458" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+2}-7x\\cdot {3}^{x+2}+12\\cdot {3}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-7x+12){3}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-7x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=3,4.$\n\n- The equation ${3}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=3,4,$ and their product is $3\\cdot 4=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33737a4b-5342-5565-914a-cb6438076b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352043} {:answer/id #uuid "380810d5-4536-54a8-96a6-03ca61571cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352085} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84059a-bf3e-5ad8-848d-de47fb5fcbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352275} {:answer/id #uuid "8597e99b-a624-5fb1-9f86-e86812b0613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352731} {:answer/id #uuid "da180e83-5bee-502b-9c12-ae8aed810433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da180e83-5bee-502b-9c12-ae8aed810433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a5b309-fce0-58d4-9cb6-f4a8f8750bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353852}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "395668d0-2b32-5884-a30f-d67a196224fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88460" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745439dc-de09-51c8-bbdb-eedea4d0f155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386573} {:answer/id #uuid "617f1c2d-99ac-5b8a-931f-683ccf1893b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386574} {:answer/id #uuid "db8e533b-8461-5017-a4f6-47771a90f0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386575} {:answer/id #uuid "e510aa7f-7425-5853-824c-91e79e70849b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386576} {:answer/id #uuid "2d786935-adc1-5afa-a4cc-25f126fdd263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e510aa7f-7425-5853-824c-91e79e70849b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386576} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a99769-405d-5ab0-ad5f-e1bf8543c410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11afc858-764d-5d9d-a321-3ddf5528f806" :question/math-academy-id "q-110801" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2-x}-{7}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1)\\cdot {7}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $-1\\cdot 1=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34bd077-9be1-5cd3-be13-0e0181077e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394774} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5c3e71-7f6d-594f-a826-d9fafbec86ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394775} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd7941b-aaf2-5165-bdc1-54cb7f6a2561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394776} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f535b7-4732-53ed-9c80-3f234b68b109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ffcbe70-bf08-5ff6-83d7-c6b4c28a448a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394777} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a33ee9e-e01b-57c4-b697-df179407774b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394773}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d2284d9-59bd-591b-a5ed-82b600cb5dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-88454" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-25\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-25)\\cdot {4}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-25=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-25=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+5)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,5,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot 5=-25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef56dae1-235e-58a4-9d49-dac58019c11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394781} {:answer/id #uuid "5d542604-cc75-5fe8-a314-152f5e0d0baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394782} {:answer/id #uuid "98750137-8ecd-58a9-830f-28a32bdedfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394783} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e12eec-8e62-581b-b20f-14c8b66d8602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394784} {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57fa678f-41a4-5b0a-9cfb-0034cbdf2533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394785} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16451bc-a0a9-548c-ba07-f9abe1416b11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394780}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d7afbe-c052-55bc-910e-6663c48abfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88459" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+12x\\cdot {2}^{x-1}+10\\cdot {2}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x+10){2}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+12x+10=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n2(x+1)(x+5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,-1.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-5,-1,$ and their product is $(-5)\\cdot (-1)=5.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9a307c1f-ecef-5a41-99c7-d97e38fc1f53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154596} {:answer-field/id #uuid "f4c86233-fc69-5709-aa26-6ecdc11cb602" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154597}] :question/id #uuid "556ae9cc-58db-5aae-8b6b-3fad8cb4fd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-237949" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $3x^{2} \\cdot e^{x + 5} - 2 \\cdot e^{x + 5} = 5x \\cdot e^{x + 5}$ are$x =$\n{{field-1}} and $x =$\n{{field-2}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b43031-2c1c-59db-af71-4eb4ae08bd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156676} {:answer/id #uuid "be3a38b8-2721-5192-a7d7-1631d8e2ec75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156677} {:answer/id #uuid "e7221e11-3e21-59f9-8c1b-404d52c225b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156678} {:answer/id #uuid "78657c4e-4d87-503c-8dd3-e41bf0dfe5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156679} {:answer/id #uuid "78a10047-013f-5ebe-840f-7ccfebbae9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf427dca-9588-5c20-a1a2-8b86ed5da176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156675}] :question/id #uuid "23dd16f1-bbbd-5da8-a565-712015ada7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-88417" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x^{2}e^{x} = 25e^{x}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234470 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f70c750-2bdb-5272-875c-7d304eb180d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88472" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b02ae-d24d-54b9-bec3-9abb19ad3a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236281} {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} {:answer/id #uuid "94ecd205-8acf-5dfb-8b92-028d364421e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236761} {:answer/id #uuid "a7056fd7-4693-5e50-92d2-77473a227f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236863} {:answer/id #uuid "f1cbddf2-0764-540e-8959-a9fe3253a384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b561d1f-b212-5afc-9626-cc8d7995672a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39d52e4f-2b82-55ff-af5e-28f93f87917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100797" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e24fbc0-aab2-5aed-8f87-83e63dcd458f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e24fbc0-aab2-5aed-8f87-83e63dcd458f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291104} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf820c1-3999-5a03-a5c2-b46691647cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f197341a-46a2-5a13-8785-e112d311489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f197341a-46a2-5a13-8785-e112d311489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291648} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e40b27-2945-566a-ac76-7521545ce25f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c61f15b5-8695-5a43-8d54-6ddb2e0a3852" :question/math-academy-id "q-264786" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}=50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}-50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{2x-5}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+5)(x-5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{2x-5}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{2x-5}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/i